  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Closed Sources — Purchase Technical Data", "technique": "T1597.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 09:02:11 CEST, Suricata logged direct HTTP requests from data.srcip=45.83.12.7 hitting data.dstip=10.108.57.184 on the internal admin portal (port 8080, paths /admin /mgmt /admin/login /admin/dashboard). What makes this anomalous: there is NO preceding subnet scan, NO ICMP sweep, NO TCP port sweep, NO service discovery against 10.108.57.0/24 — the external source went STRAIGHT to one internal IP, one port, and the exact admin paths on the first connection. The data.http.user_agent string mimics a legitimate corporate admin browser (Chrome/119.0.6045.105 on Windows 10). Direct first-contact knowledge of an internal-only IP, an internal-only port, and the precise admin path is the behavioural signature of an adversary acting on PURCHASED technical reconnaissance data (asset inventory + config document obtained from a closed/private market), not on self-collected scan results.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP alerts with data.srcip=45.83.12.7 + data.dstip=10.108.57.184 + data.url containing /admin and /api/v2 paths, with NO matching ET SCAN signatures (2010935 / 2010937 / 2024364) from the same data.srcip in the preceding 30 days. Wazuh custom rule 'direct internal admin portal access from external IP with no scan precursor' fired and attached MITRE T1597.002.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Acting on Purchased Credentials", "technique": "T1597.002", "what_happened": "At 09:05:30 CEST the same data.srcip=45.83.12.7 performed single-shot authentication attempts against 10.108.57.184:8080/admin/login using service-account credentials (svc-vpn, admin, netadmin) — one POST per account, no brute-force iteration, no username enumeration, no password spraying. This single-shot pattern indicates the attacker already POSSESSED specific valid-looking credentials (purchased in the same intel package) rather than guessing them. Each auth attempt was immediately followed by a CVE-2022-40684 FortiGate auth-bypass probe carrying the X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1 / X-Real-IP: 127.0.0.1 header trick against /api/v2/cmdb/system/admin/<user>.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP POST alerts to data.url '/admin/login' from data.srcip=45.83.12.7, immediately correlated with HTTP requests to data.url '/api/v2/cmdb/system/admin/*' carrying spoofed localhost forwarding headers. Suricata signature for CVE-2022-40684 FortiGate authentication bypass fired. rule.mitre.id T1597.002 + T1190 attached.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:05:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access / Exploit Public-Facing Application", "technique": "T1190", "what_happened": "At 09:09:00 CEST data.srcip=45.83.12.7 ran firmware-version-specific CVE exploitation against 10.108.57.184. Two distinct CVE chains were attempted in sequence: (a) CVE-2022-40684 FortiGate auth bypass against the FortiGate REST API endpoints /api/v2/cmdb/system/admin, /api/v2/cmdb/firewall/policy, /api/v2/cmdb/vpn.ssl/settings, /api/v2/monitor/system/status, /api/v2/monitor/vpn/ssl; and (b) CVE-2018-13379 FortiGate SSL-VPN path traversal against /remote/fgt_lang?lang=/../../../..//////////dev/cmdb/sslvpn_websession and /remote/fortisslvpn/login. The attacker targeted the EXACT FortiGate 60F firmware 7.2.3 product line — knowing the precise appliance model and firmware version without any prior fingerprinting is only possible if the model/firmware was contained in the purchased config document.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signatures for CVE-2022-40684 and CVE-2018-13379 fired with data.srcip=45.83.12.7 + data.dstip=10.108.57.184. data.url values match the FortiGate REST API and SSL-VPN traversal patterns. rule.mitre.id T1190 attached. The exploitation directly follows step 2's purchased-credential auth on the same target with no intervening discovery activity.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:09:00+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Acting on Purchased Config Document", "technique": "T1597.002", "what_happened": "At 09:15:00 CEST data.srcip=45.83.12.7 probed a precise list of FortiGate management endpoints on 10.108.57.184:8080 — /system/status, /system/interface, /vpn/status, /firewall/policy, /log/traffic/local, /monitor/router/ipv4, /report/adom/root/run — replaying a session cookie obtained earlier in the chain. The attacker did NOT crawl or spider the application to discover these paths; the endpoint list was walked directly and in order, which is the signature of a config/management-path inventory taken straight from the purchased documentation package.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP alerts with data.srcip=45.83.12.7 + data.dstip=10.108.57.184 + data.url iterating the exact management endpoint list with a replayed cookie. No directory-brute-force signature (no 404-storm, no wordlist pattern) precedes the endpoint access. Wazuh correlation rule aggregated steps 1-4 from the same data.srcip within a ~16-minute window and tagged the group T1597.002 + T1190, rule.level 13.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:15:00+0200"}
  ],
